    "content": "Temporary Protected Status (TPS) is a U.S. immigration benefit granted by the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) to eligible nationals of designated countries facing ongoing armed conflict, environmental disasters, or extraordinary, temporary conditions. It prevents deportation and allows beneficiaries to live and work in the U.S. legally for a limited time.Key details about TPS include:Benefits: Holders receive temporary protection from removal, work authorization (Employment Authorization Document), and travel authorization.Eligibility: Applicants must be from a designated country, have been continuously physically present in the U.S. since the designated date, and file during the registration period.Limitations: TPS is temporary and does not automatically lead to lawful permanent residence (a \"green card\") or citizenship.Designated Countries: As of early 2026, countries with TPS include Afghanistan, Cameroon, El Salvador, Haiti, Honduras, Nepal, Nicaragua, Somalia, South Sudan, Sudan, Syria, Ukraine, Venezuela, and others.Individuals can apply using Form I-821, Application for Temporary Protected Status through USCIS.",
